{"type":"event","event":{"type":"tool_completed","toolCallId":"call_c6c8928513234f4d83a3faab","toolName":"read_file","result":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 1179 lines\n1: # dao.stream.host-boundary: dao.stream wherever the boundary can be an IO action\n2: \n3: The governing test is the owner's: **dao.stream is used wherever a host\n4: boundary can be an IO action; a pure, total, synchronous function on the same\n5: host may be a direct call.** This document's commissionable scope is that\n6: rule (§2) and the pure-primitive slices H0-H4 (§8): freeze and allowlist; the\n7: `dao.host.*` integer, float, number, bytes and text profile; migration of\n8: every consumer; hash and signature entries; the portable Jing CBOR writer.\n9: Entropy and scheduling, the durable-storage seam, the parser stage and the\n10: stream conformance suite are **deferred** to a separate design (§9) and are\n11: not commissionable here.\n12: \n13: Status: design only; nothing commissioned. **Revision r5**, 2026-10-20, by\n14: the Architect (claude-fable-5-1). **Review status: SIGNED OFF WITH CHANGES\n15: by a non-Claude architect (gpt-6-astra, 2026-10-20,\n16: `collab/1792508286000-architect-host-boundary-review-r4.gpt-6-astra.findings.md`)\n17: for the narrowed scope only, §2 plus H0-H4; H5-H8 (§9) are not signed off\n18: and remain deferred.** The four mechanical corrections M1-M4 that sign-off\n19: required were applied in r5 (rationale\n20: `collab/1792508512000-architect-host-boundary-r5.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`)\n21: and should be confirmed by a non-Claude reviewer before H0 is dispatched.\n22: Lineage: r1 rationale\n23: `collab/1792495909000-architect-host-boundary.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`;\n24: r1 review NOT SIGNED OFF\n25: (`collab/1792498045000-architect-host-boundary-review.gpt-6-astra.findings.md`,\n26: A1-A8); r2 rationale `collab/1792502807000-...-r2.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`;\n27: r2 review NOT SIGNED OFF\n28: (`collab/1792503557000-architect-host-boundary-review-r2.gpt-6-astra.findings.md`,\n29: R1-R6); r3 rationale and disposition\n30: `collab/1792506992000-architect-host-boundary-r3.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`;\n31: r3 scoped review NOT SIGNED OFF\n32: (`collab/1792507576000-architect-host-boundary-review-r3.gpt-6-astra.findings.md`,\n33: N1-N6); **r4** answers N1-N6 within the unchanged scope (rationale\n34: `collab/1792507856000-architect-host-boundary-r4.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`).\n35: Owner decision for r3 (verbatim): \"yes, proceed with the split\". No code was\n36: changed; every claim about the tree is labelled. **Because this document was\n37: written by a Claude model, a non-Claude architect must sign off this final\n38: text (§2 plus H0-H4) before anything is commissioned from it** (§14).\n39: \n40: Owner direction, verbatim. Original: \"maybe this is an opportunity to stop\n41: and refactor so that the dao.stream is the boundary that crosses over host\n42: specific dependency. once that is done, the only thing needs porting is\n43: dao.stream implementations\". Refined: \"maybe my dao.stream ABI framing has to\n44: be refined.  when a host-boundary is cross should it be used. when its on the\n45: same host, then direct function call is fine. In other words, when the host\n46: boundary can be an IO monad then dao.stream is used. If it cannot be a monad\n47: than dao.stream doesnt have to be used\". Asked which formulation governs:\n48: \"yes, test 2 governs\".\n49: \n50: Authorities:\n51: - [datom.world.md](./datom.world.md) `:43-48`, `:70`.\n52: - [datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) §3 custody; §4 machine-internal\n53:   work executes inside the instance; §5 the floor and internal linkage; §6\n54:   effects out, events in; §7 adapter discipline (`:143-148`). §12 is an\n55:   owner proposal to amend it; nothing here edits it.\n56: - [dao.stream.md](./dao.stream.md): the contract; cursors are non-destructive\n57:   (`src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc:159-173`, V via the r2 review).\n58: - [dao.stream.wasm.md](./dao.stream.wasm.md) §1 `:29-52`: seven imports.\n59: - [dao.stream.codec.md](./dao.stream.codec.md): the codec track; untouched\n60:   here (§8.1).\n61: - [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5; [dao.jing.md](./dao.jing.md);\n62:   [dao.jing.cbor.md](./dao.jing.cbor.md).\n63: \n64: Labels: V = verified by reading this tree on 2026-10-20 (line cites); R =\n65: read, computed by script, or inferred, not run under a compiler; P =\n66: proposed. Nothing was executed on a lane.\n67: \n68: Standalone: a future host lane is an example consumer, never a dependency.\n69: \n70: ---\n71: \n72: ## 1. Problem\n73: \n74: V: the portable core (`src/cljc`, 183 files) reaches hosts two ways and the\n75: doctrine sanctions only one. The sanctioned way is the transports' pattern:\n76: a portable core plus a per-host seam value the composition hands in\n77: (`yin.repl.host/websocket`, `host.cljc:20-37`; `datagram.jvm/bind!`,\n78: `jvm.clj:22-34`). The other way is host contact inside portable files: reader\n79: arms reaching a host library, and resolved calls to host-backed core\n80: functions that compile everywhere and still touch the world.\n81: \n82: The in-scope files (V, `rg -c '#\\?'`; what the arms reach):\n83: \n84: +-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n85: | File                              | `#?`  | Host contact (V)                                                                                        |\n86: +===================================+=======+=========================================================================================================+\n87: | `dao/jing/cbor.cljc` (1562 lines) | 52    | Big-integer helpers (`:106-254`); `Float64`/`Rational`/`Decimal` carriers (`:342-460`); JVM             |\n88: |                                   |       | `BigDecimal`/`Ratio` construction and access (`:483-632`); float bits (`:525-541`); writer selection    |\n89: |                                   |       | (`:58-60`: Boring on JVM and JS, the Dart `cbor` package).                                              |\n90: +-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n91: | `yin/vm/integer/host.cljc` (299)  | 30    | 25 public functions (`:41-298`): carrier recognition, promotion, 13 big operations.                     |\n92: +-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n93: | `dao/jing.cljc` (629)             | 12    | BLAKE3 and SHA-256 libraries (`:32-40, :59-135`); an opaque sentinel (`:43-51`).                        |\n94: +-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n95: | `yin/vm/linker/sign.cljc` (274)   | 16    | Ed25519 libraries; `jvm-pair` replays a given seed through a `SecureRandom` proxy (`:75-87`, V:         |\n96: |                                   |       | deterministic, not fresh entropy); `generate` draws fresh entropy (`:114-124`, deferred, §9).           |\n97: +-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n98: | `dao/jing/cbor/boring.cljc`       | 14    | The two wire writers (`:cljd nil`-gated Boring wrapper; 107-line Dart twin).                            |\n99: | (132), `src/cljd/dao/jing/cbor/cl |       |                                                                                                         |\n100: | jd.cljd`                          |       |                                                                                                         |\n101: +-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n102: | `yin/vm/debruijn.cljc` (16)       | 16    | Bytes predicate (`:167`), char access (`:241`), NFC (`:310-321`, Unicode 16.0 owner-settled), float     |\n103: |                                   |       | bits (`:855-885`), carrier tests (`:1255-1257`).                                                        |\n104: +-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n105: | `yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc` (13)  | 13    | Float64 little-endian hex (`:232-242`); JVM big-integer predicate (`:315-317`).                         |\n106: +-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n107: | `yin/vm/data.cljc` (7),           | 7+9   | Code units and `units->string` (`:119-173`); `integral` (`:77-92`); `Closure`/`Continuation` equality,  |\n108: | `yin/vm/values.cljc` (9)          |       | hash and print arms (`:39-119`).                                                                        |\n109: +-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n110: | `dao/stream/chunks.cljc` (5),     | 5+4+4 | `Uint8List` length, alloc, slice, copy (`chunks.cljc:8-32`); base64 libraries; host-bytes predicate     |\n111: | `base64.cljc`, `codec.cljc`       |       | (`codec.cljc:45`, codec-track file, §8.1).                                                              |\n112: +-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n113: | `dao/jing/dht.cljc` (10),         | 10+14 | Bytes helpers (`dht.cljc:100-121`); bytes length (`linker.cljc:1232`); `host-bytes`/`utf8`              |\n114: | `yin/vm/linker.cljc`, `datagram`  |       | (`sign.cljc:55-66`); byte length (`datagram.cljc:63-66`).                                               |\n115: +-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n116: | `dao/data/arrays.cljc` (21)       | 21    | Host arrays, `^long` hints, `parallelSort` (`:145`).                                                    |\n117: +-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n118: \n119: Out of scope here and listed in §9: `dao/jing/file.cljc`, `dao/space/store/\n120: fs.cljc`, `dao/space/store.cljc`, `dao/stream/journal/file.cljc`,\n121: `dao/data/btree/storage.cljc` (storage); `dao/jing/content/async.cljc`,\n122: `dao/jing/coordinate.cljc:80` and `src/clj/dao/jing/content/driver.clj`\n123: (scheduling, blocking driver); every entropy site; `yang/python/antlr/\n124: parser.cljc` and the `yang.antlr.cst` hosts (parser stage).\n125: \n126: V, the duplication: `yin.vm.integer.host` says it \"does not reach into\n127: `dao.jing.cbor`'s private big-integer helpers\" (`host.cljc:23-25`); `cbor.cljc`\n128: keeps its own (`:112-216`). Two shims, one contract.\n129: \n130: ## 2. The governing rule\n131: \n132: ### 2.1 The boundary, defined first (review R1)\n133: \n134: **The machine** is the running instance and the state it owns: its values,\n135: and the mutable runtime state its own code created and no other party can\n136: observe or change: atoms and volatiles, host arrays and byte arrays it\n137: allocated, a B-tree's weak or soft references, the collector that clears\n138: them, thread-local locks between threads the driver created. **The world** is\n139: everything else: files, sockets, other processes, the operating system's\n140: clock, entropy sources, environment, the terminal, and any other instance.\n141: \n142: A host contact is **external** when it observes or changes the world. It is\n143: **internal** when it is a function of the machine's values and owned runtime\n144: state only.\n145: \n146: ### 2.2 Statement\n147: \n148: **An external contact uses `dao.stream`: it can be an IO action. An internal\n149: contact that is a pure, total, synchronous function of its arguments, or a\n150: declared mutation of an owned argument (§2.4), may be a direct call and is\n151: then an entry of the primitive profile (§4.3).**\n152: \n153: This is the owner's test 2. Same-host-ness is not the criterion: a same-host\n154: file read is external and is a stream; a hash from a library linked into the\n155: instance is internal and is a direct call.\n156: \n157: ### 2.3 \"Can be an IO action\", operationally\n158: \n159: A contact can be an IO action when any of these holds **with respect to the\n160: world** (§2.1):\n161: \n162: +----+--------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n163: | #  | Property                       | Meaning and the doctrine that names it                                                                     |\n164: +====+================================+============================================================================================================+\n165: | 1  | Observation of the world       | The result depends on world state: a file, a socket, another process, the environment, a resource's        |\n166: |    |                                | current condition. `abi.md` §5: \"world facts that shipped code cannot compute\".                            |\n167: +----+--------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n168: | 2  | Effect on the world            | Calling it changes world state: bytes on disk, a packet sent, a lock taken, text printed.                  |\n169: |    |                                | `datom.world.md:46`: \"Side effects must appear as stream emissions.\"                                       |\n170: +----+--------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n171: | 3  | World nondeterminism           | Two calls with equal arguments and equal owned state may answer differently because of the world: entropy, |\n172: |    |                                | a clock, an OS-assigned pid, a process-wide counter shared with code the machine does not own.             |\n173: +----+--------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n174: | 4  | Asynchrony                     | The answer is not available at return, or completion invokes something later: a timer, a thread, a         |\n175: |    |                                | promise. `dao.stream.md`: \"No operation waits\"; `abi.md` §6: a callback is an event on a stream.           |\n176: +----+--------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n177: | 5  | World-dependent failure        | Whether it completes depends on the world (disk full, peer gone), not on the input. `dao.stream.md` Result |\n178: |    |                                | Convention: such outcomes are data.                                                                        |\n179: +----+--------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n180: | 6  | Another process or host        | The work runs in a different process, machine or trust domain. `abi.md` §3.                                |\n181: +----+--------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n182: \n183: A contact cannot be an IO action when none holds. Then it is a function of\n184: the machine's values and owned state, computed synchronously in the same\n185: instance, whose only failures are functions of the input, answered as data\n186: or a refusal. Linking a library to compute it is internal linkage (`abi.md`\n187: §5 `:80-81`); executing it is machine-internal work (`abi.md` §4 `:60-62`).\n188: The basis of the primitive profile is therefore **internality plus purity,\n189: totality and synchrony over the declared domain**, with mutation admitted\n190: only as §2.4 declares it.\n191: \n192: A read of owned runtime state that may differ between calls (a weak\n193: reference after a collection, an atom another owned thread advanced) fails\n194: none of the six properties: it is a fact about the machine, not the world.\n195: It is a **runtime prerequisite** (table C), neither IO nor a profile entry.\n196: r2 classified it as both; that contradiction is withdrawn.\n197: \n198: ### 2.4 Ownership and mutation laws for the profile\n199: \n200: The profile's purity claim is exact, not blanket:\n201: \n202: - **Pure entries** (marked P in table A) are deterministic, total over their\n203:   declared domain, synchronous, and allocate only fresh values; equal\n204:   arguments give equal results on every host.\n205: - **Mutating entries** (marked M: `bytes/set!`, `bytes/copy!`, `arrays/aset`,\n206:   `arrays/acopy`, `arrays/asort`) mutate **exactly the argument named as the\n207:   target** and nothing else, and never retain it. Return values are per\n208:   entry, not uniform: `aset` returns the assigned value (Clojure's `aset`\n209:   semantics, V `arrays.cljc:50-57`, unchanged); `set!`, `copy!` and `acopy`\n210:   return nil; `asort` returns the sorted array. Overlapping `copy!` and\n211:   `acopy` behave as memmove (V: `acopy` already handles same-array overlap,\n212:   `:97-105`). **Prevalidation**: every index and length is checked before\n213:   any write, so a refused call leaves the target byte-identical. This is a\n214:   **behaviour change** on JS and Dart, where today's `acopy` loop writes\n215:   element by element and can write before meeting an invalid index\n216:   (`arrays.cljc:97-105`, V; the JVM's `System/arraycopy` checks first); it\n217:   is assigned to H2 with a no-partial-write test (table D).\n218: - **Aliasing**: `bytes/slice`, `arrays/asub`, `arrays/aclone`, `from-vector`\n219:   always return a fresh value; no entry returns a view onto an argument.\n220:   Nothing else is promised about copying: a value appended to a stream is\n221:   **not** copied by the stream in general. The ring buffer stores the value\n222:   object itself and reads return it (`ringbuffer.cljc:124-131`, V), and the\n223:   ABI permits in-instance streams with no crossing and no serialization\n224:   (`dao.stream.wasm.md:22-27`). Aliasing across a stream is therefore\n225:   possible, and the rule below is what makes it safe.\n226: - **Exclusive mutation right, ownership transfer, and freezing\n227:   publication.** A mutable profile value (bytes, a host array) has exactly\n228:   one party with the right to mutate it. Two distinct events move or end\n229:   that right:\n230:   - **Ownership transfer** (the right moves, the value stays mutable): when\n231:     a profile entry marked \"fresh\" (`alloc`, `slice`, `from-vector`,\n232:     `magnitude-bytes`, `anew`, `aclone`, `asub`, `aconcat`, `ainsert-at`,\n233:     `aremove-at`, `from-coll`, `utf8-encode`) returns a value, the **caller**\n234:     holds the exclusive mutation right from that return, and may `set!`,\n235:     `copy!`, `aset`, `acopy` or `asort` it. Returning fresh storage is not\n236:     publication.\n237:   - **Freezing publication** (the right ends for everyone): appending the\n238:     value to a stream (in-memory or not); placing it inside a value that is\n239:     appended; handing it to a seam or a content handle. On publication the\n240:     value is **frozen**: no party, the publisher included, may mutate it\n241:     afterwards, and a reader may not mutate what it reads. Freezing is\n242:     transitive: a collection is frozen with every mutable value nested in\n243:     it. A party that needs a mutable copy of a frozen value takes `slice`,\n244:     `aclone` or `from-vector`, and then holds the right to that copy.\n245: - **Obligations enforceable within H0-H4**: (i) every P entry leaves every\n246:   argument byte-identical, asserted by a property test in H1 over the bytes,\n247:   text, float and integer namespaces; (ii) every M entry mutates only its\n248:   named target and only after prevalidation, asserted in H1 (bytes) and H2\n249:   (arrays); (iii) the Jing writer (H4) never mutates its input and returns\n250:   fresh bytes, asserted by re-encoding the same input twice and comparing\n251:   the input's bytes before and after; (iv) the callers of M entries inside\n252:   the adopted closure are enumerated and audited in H2 for the freeze rule:\n253:   `dao.data.btree*` (owned node arrays, never appended), `dao.stream.chunks`\n254:   (`absorb` into a fresh buffer, `chunks.cljc:27-34`), `dao.jing.cbor`\n255:   (`magnitude-bytes` and the byte-string assembly at `:254,1313-1317`, fresh\n256:   output), `yin.vm.linker.sign` (`:82`, a fresh seed copy); the list is the\n257:   acceptance, and a new caller fails the H0 gate's list 2 until reviewed.\n258:   What is **not** enforceable here: a reader mutating a frozen value it\n259:   obtained from a stream; that is a composition obligation stated by this\n260:   rule and left to review until the deferred conformance suite (§9.4).\n261: \n262: ### 2.5 Borderline cases, decided\n263: \n264: +-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n265: | Case                              | Reasoning under §2.3                                                                                    | Verdict    |\n266: +===================================+=========================================================================================================+============+\n267: | UTF-8 decode, CBOR decode, base64 | Fallible, but the failure is a function of the input (property 5 does not hold); deterministic,         | Direct     |\n268: | decode, NFC normalization         | synchronous. Malformed input is a refusal value. NFC depends on a Unicode version, which the profile    | call       |\n269: |                                   | pins (16.0, V `debruijn.cljc:314`).                                                                     |            |\n270: +-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n271: | Hashing, signing, seed replay in  | Deterministic functions of bytes (and of the seed). The library is linkage. They execute in the same    | Direct     |\n272: | a library (BLAKE3, SHA-256,       | instance; for wasm, compiled in, never imported (§7). `sign.cljc:75-87`'s `SecureRandom` proxy replays  | call, in-  |\n273: | Ed25519; `sign.cljc:75-87`)       | the given seed bytes and is deterministic (V, R3 correction).                                           | instance   |\n274: +-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n275: | Fresh entropy, identities, key    | Property 3. Deferred design (§9); the profile contains no generator.                                    | Stream;    |\n276: | generation (`sign.cljc:114-124`)  |                                                                                                         | deferred   |\n277: +-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n278: | Time; scheduling; the JVM         | Properties 1, 3, 4. `coordinate.cljc:80` selects `driver.clj:169-206`, which reads the clock and sleeps | Stream /   |\n279: | blocking content driver           | (V): JVM-lane IO inside a portable composition. Deferred (§9).                                          | driver;    |\n280: |                                   |                                                                                                         | deferred   |\n281: +-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n282: | The Python parser                 | In-process, synchronous, deterministic over source text and the pinned grammar: internal, direct call.  | Either;    |\n283: |                                   | As another process: property 6, a stream. The stage shape (`parser.cljc:65-75`) serves both. Deferred.  | deferred   |\n284: +-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n285: | Same-host file read, lock, fsync, | Properties 1, 2, 5. External though same-host. Deferred (§9).                                           | Stream;    |\n286: | rename, directory listing         |                                                                                                         | deferred   |\n287: +-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n288: | Weak and soft references,         | Owned runtime state (§2.1). Not IO, not a profile entry: table C. The B-tree's default reference mode   | Runtime    |\n289: | atomics, `locking`, atoms         | is `:soft` on the JVM and `:strong` on JS and Dart (V `btree.cljc:39-44`); correctness is independent   | prereq.    |\n290: |                                   | of the mode, only retention differs.                                                                    |            |\n291: +-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n292: | Host `=`, `hash`, `compare`,      | Internal and pure per host, not identical across hosts for mixed types and floats. A portability hazard | Dialect    |\n293: | `sort`, `str` of a double, regex  | only where persisted bytes depend on it; those sites sort by canonical bytes (`cbor.cljc:1070,1177`,    | semantics  |\n294: |                                   | `ucf/rows.cljc:139`, `debruijn.cljc:921,929`). Regex sites (`custody.cljc:53`, `datagram.cljc:86-100`,  |            |\n295: |                                   | `jing.cljc:107-111,216-221`, `fs.cljc:105`) match ASCII grammars identical on every host.               |            |\n296: +-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n297: | `gensym` in a compiler            | A process-wide counter shared with code the machine does not own: property 3 in a weak form. Deferred   | Deferred   |\n298: |                                   | (§9), with the hygiene requirement the r2 review added.                                                 |            |\n299: +-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n300: \n301: ### 2.6 Where the owner's original sentence is and is not met\n302: \n303: Met for every external contact: after this design and the deferred one, a\n304: host's world contact is the stream seams and its `main`. Not met literally:\n305: a port also implements the primitive profile and meets table C, because the\n306: dialect's own carriers cannot be supplied by a stream and must not be. The\n307: profile is exact, enumerated and oracle-checked, so that part is a list, not\n308: a design.\n309: \n310: ## 3. Classification over the adopted closure\n311: \n312: ### 3.1 The transitive closure, per lane (R, computed)\n313: \n314: Method: a require-form traversal from `yin.repl.main`, choosing each lane's\n315: reader arms (`:cljd` also reads `:clj`) and resolving each namespace to its\n316: file under `src/<lane>` first (shadow resolution: `yin.repl.host` is\n317: `host.cljc` on the JVM and the `host.cljs`/`host.cljd` shadows elsewhere),\n318: then `src/cljc`. It is not a compiler-resolved call graph: it can\n319: over-include a namespace required but unused on a path, cannot see dynamic\n320: loading, and **can under-include** when its reader-arm parsing fails; the\n321: r3 script did exactly that (it dropped requires inside `#?@` arms and\n322: reported 141/140/139). The r3 claim that it \"cannot under-include\" is\n323: withdrawn. The reconciled totals, counted by **file** and confirmed by the\n324: r3 reviewer's independent traversal (R):\n325: \n326: +-------+-------+----------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n327: | Lane  | Total | Shared   | Lane-specific files (V, the files exist)                                                                            |\n328: +=======+=======+==========+=====================================================================================================================+\n329: | clj   | 142   | 133      | `clj/dao/jing/content/driver.clj` (blocking driver: clock, sleep, `locking`), `clj/dao/stream/datagram/jvm.clj`,    |\n330: |       |       |          | `clj/dao/stream/waitset/driver.clj`, `clj/dao/stream/ws/jvm.clj` (clock, `CompletableFuture`), `clj/yang/antlr/     |\n331: |       |       |          | cst.clj`, `clj/yin/repl/host/jvm.clj`, `cljc/yin/repl/host.cljc` (the JVM selector),                                |\n332: |       |       |   